+99_SANDBOX/SpaceBunnyAlpha_Review_01:440:- **It carries the most information per minute.** It simultaneously proves: static ARMv6 binaries execute on this CPU; the SD card is a viable payload location; Wi-Fi association and a routable IP work from a root shell (an item your earlier capture left unexplained); the whole "does a real Linux program run here" question, which currently gates five separate roadmap items.
+99_SANDBOX/SpaceBunnyAlpha_Review_01:441:- **It de-risks everything downstream.** If a static hard-float ARMv6 binary runs from `/data/local/tmp`, it runs from a loop-mounted ext3 image on the SD card, and the chroot milestone becomes plumbing rather than a compatibility gamble. If it *fails*, you learn that in ten minutes on the host, not after a weekend of rootfs assembly.
